"Räty–Sudakov–Tomon conjecture on graph surplus and least eigenvalues | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/405977/raty-sudakov-tomon-conjecture-on-graph-surplus-and-least-eig)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; # Räty–Sudakov–Tomon conjecture on graph surplus and least eigenvalues ... An October 2026 preprint claims to prove the conjecture with stronger bounds, but the result has not been independently verified.\n\n# Räty–Sudakov–Tomon conjecture on graph surplus and least eigenvalues\n\nFor every $\\varepsilon>0$ε>0, there exist constants $c_{\\varepsilon}>0$c ε​>0 and $n_{0}(\\varepsilon)$n 0​(ε) such that, for every $n\\geq n_{0}(\\varepsilon)$n≥n 0​(ε) and every $n$n-vertex graph $G$G that is $\\varepsilon$ε-far in edit distance from every disjoint union of cliques, one has $\\operatorname{disc}^{+}(G)\\geq c_{\\varepsilon}n^{5/4}$disc+(G)≥c ε​n 5/4, $\\operatorname{sp}(G)\\geq c_{\\varepsilon}n^{5/4}$sp(G)≥c ε​n 5/4, and $|\\lambda_{n}(G)|\\geq c_{\\varepsilon}n^{1/4}$∣λ n​(G)∣≥c ε​n 1/4, where $\\lambda_{n}(G)$λ n​(G) is the least adjacency eigenvalue of $G$G.\n\nReferences\n\n  * Lower bounds for the magnitude of the minimum eigenvalue of graphs with applications to MaxCut and Chowla's cosine problem — arXiv — Fredy Yip\n\nSource: arXiv\n\ndiscrepancy-theoryextremal-graph-theoryspectral-graph-theory\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 4 weeks ago\n\nOpen · solution reported\n\nAn October 2026 preprint claims to prove the conjecture with stronger bounds, but the result has not been independently verified.\n\nRäty, Sudakov, and Tomon conjectured that graphs far from cluster-like structures must have large positive discrepancy, surplus, and least-eigenvalue magnitude. The conjecture connects structural distance from Turán graphs or disjoint unions of cliques with quantitative spectral and extremal bounds.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * Jin, Milojević, Tomon, and Zhang proved a weaker bound $\\operatorname{disc}^{+}(G)\\geq \\Omega_{\\delta}(n^{1+c})$disc+(G)≥Ω δ​(n 1+c) for some absolute $c>0$c>0.\n  * Janzer, Tomon, and Yip proved $\\operatorname{disc}^{+}(G)\\geq n^{5/4-o(1)}$disc+(G)≥n 5/4−o(1).\n  * The same work established $\\operatorname{sp}(G)\\geq n^{5/4-o(1)}$sp(G)≥n 5/4−o(1) for graphs far from disjoint unions of cliques.\n\nOctober 2026 claimed resolution\n\nAn October 1, 2026 report on Fredy Yip's preprint claims fixed-constant bounds $\\operatorname{disc}^{+}(G),\\operatorname{sp}(G)\\geq \\Omega_{\\epsilon}(n^{5/4})$disc+(G),sp(G)≥Ω ϵ​(n 5/4) and $|\\lambda_n|\\geq \\Omega_{\\epsilon}(n^{1/4})$∣λ n​∣≥Ω ϵ​(n 1/4), removing the earlier $o(1)$o(1) losses. Under an $n^{-o(1)}$n−o(1) distance assumption it also reports $n^{1/4-o(1)}$n 1/4−o(1) and $n^{5/4-o(1)}$n 5/4−o(1) bounds. These are claims from an unrefereed preprint.\n\nCurrent status (as of October 2026): Earlier asymptotic bounds are established, while the full fixed-constant conjecture is only claimed in an unrefereed preprint and remains unverified.\n\nSources\n\n  * arxiv.org ↗\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrankl's conjecture on shattering in antichains — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/369576/frankl-s-conjecture-on-shattering-in-antichains)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Frankl's conjecture on shattering in antichains ... A family $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq 2^{[n]}$F⊆2[n] is an antichain if no two members satisfy $F\\subseteq F'$F⊆F′. ... Posted by arXiv 6 days ago\n\nFrankl's conjecture on shattering in antichains\n\nLet $2^{[n]}$2[n] denote the family of all subsets of $[n]$[n]. A family $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq 2^{[n]}$F⊆2[n] is an antichain if no two members satisfy $F\\subseteq F'$F⊆F′.\n\nFrankl's conjecture. Let $k$k be a non-negative integer and $n\\geqslant 2k$n⩾2 k. If $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq 2^{[n]}$F⊆2[n] is an antichain with $\\mathcal{F}\\not\\rightarrow(k+1,2^{k+1})$F→(k+1,2 k+1), then\n\n$$|\\mathcal{F}|\\leqslant\\binom{n}{k}.$$∣F∣⩽(k n​).\n\nFrankl proved the conjecture for $k\\leqslant 2$k⩽2, and Anstee and Sali proved it for $k=3$k=3; the general case remains open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nMingze Li, Jie Ma and Mingyuan Rong, “Recent advances in arrow relations and traces of sets”, arXiv:2507.23375 (2025).\n\nextremal-combinatoricsset-systemsshattering\n\n0\n\nPosted by arXiv 6 days ago\n\n## Research in progress\n\nDurable attempts, artifacts, checks, and next steps that another researcher can continue.\n\nNo shared research yet.\n\n* * *\n\n## 0 Replies\n\n* * *\n\nSign in to reply.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPartitioning triangle-free planar graphs into a forest and a linear forest - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S019566982600079X)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Raspaud and Wang conjectured that every triangle-free planar graph can be vertex-partitioned into an independent set and a forest. ... Conjecture 1, if true, would imply many other results such as Grötzsch’s theorem, and would give positive answers to Question 28 in [16] and Question 2 in [6], which concern list colorings of triangle-free planar graphs with restrictions on the list assignments.\n\n## European Journal of Combinatorics\n\nVolume 137, September 2026, 104411\n\n# Partitioning triangle-free planar graphs into a forest and a linear forest\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2026.104411Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nRaspaud and Wang conjectured that every triangle-free planar graph can be vertex-partitioned into an independent set and a forest. Independently, Kawarabayashi and Thomassen also remarked that this might be true, after providing another proof of a result of Borodin and Glebov, showing this result for planar graphs of girth at least five. Subsequently, Dross, Montassier, and Pinlou raised the same question and proved that every triangle-free planar graph can be partitioned into a forest and another forest of maximum degree at most 5. More recently, Feghali and Šámal improved this bound on the maximum degree to 3. In this note, we further improve the result by showing that every triangle-free planar graph can be partitioned into a forest and a linear forest, that is, a forest of maximum degree at most 2.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLower bounds for the magnitude of the minimum eigenvalue of graphs with applications to MaxCut and Chowla's cosine problem (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01657)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Thu Oct  1 13:18:25 2026 ... Recent results of Janzer, Tomon and Yip allow us to directly transfer least eigenvalue estimates to the corresponding surplus estimates. Using these tools, we fully resolve a conjecture of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon.\nTitle: Lower bounds for the magnitude of the minimum eigenvalue of graphs with applications to MaxCut and Chowla's cosine problem\nAuthors: Fredy Yip\nDate: Thu Oct  1 13:18:25 2026\n\nJin, Milojević, Tomon and Zhang established a powerful recursive estimate relating the positive eigenvalues of a graph whose least eigenvalue is small in absolute value. We establish a refinement of this result, yielding improved estimates across spectral graph theory and discrepancy theory, and for Chowla's cosine problem. Recent results of Janzer, Tomon and Yip allow us to directly transfer least eigenvalue estimates to the corresponding surplus estimates. Using these tools, we fully resolve a conjecture of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon. We show that, for an $n$-vertex graph $G$ with least eigenvalue $λ_n$ and surplus $\\operatorname{sp}(G)$, if $G$ is $ε$-far from all disjoint unions of cliques, then $|λ_n|\\geq Ω_ε(n^{1/4})$ and $\\operatorname{sp}(G)\\geq Ω_ε(n^{5/4})$. Furthermore, we show that when $G$ is $n^{-o(1)}$-far from all disjoint unions of cliques, we have $|λ_n|\\geq n^{1/4 - o(1)}$ and $\\operatorname{sp}(G)\\geq n^{5/4 - o(1)}$. Finally, we show that the surplus of a $K_t$-free graph with $m$ edges is at least $m^{0.614 - o(1)}$ as $m$ tends to infinity.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOWR-2025-42 (https://publications.mfo.de/bitstream/handle/mfo/4383/OWR_2025_42.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=1)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; **Conjecture 2** (Conlon–Fox–Kwan–Sudakov [2]). *For any \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\) with either \\(k \\ge 4\\) or \\(r \\ge 3\\), every \\(k\\)-uniform hypergraph with \\(m\\) edges has an \\(r\\)-cut of surplus \\(\\Omega(m^{2/3})\\). ... In the particular case where \\(r \\in \\{k-1,k\\}\\), Räty and Tomon [6] recently improved Conlon, Fox, Kwan and Sudakov’s bound to \\(\\Omega(m^{3/5-o(1)})\\) by using spectral techniques.\n2266  Oberwolfach Report 42/2025\n\nConlon et al. [2] conjectured that this random hypergraph is asymptotically optimal whenever \\((r,k) \\neq (2,2),(2,3)\\).\n\n**Conjecture 2** (Conlon–Fox–Kwan–Sudakov [2]). *For any \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\) with either \\(k \\ge 4\\) or \\(r \\ge 3\\), every \\(k\\)-uniform hypergraph with \\(m\\) edges has an \\(r\\)-cut of surplus \\(\\Omega(m^{2/3})\\).*\n\nIn the particular case where \\(r \\in \\{k-1,k\\}\\), Räty and Tomon [6] recently improved Conlon, Fox, Kwan and Sudakov’s bound to \\(\\Omega(m^{3/5-o(1)})\\) by using spectral techniques.\n\n**Theorem 3** (Räty–Tomon [6]). *If \\(r \\in \\{k-1,k\\}\\) and \\(r \\ge 3\\), then every \\(k\\)-uniform hypergraph with \\(m\\) edges has an \\(r\\)-cut of surplus \\(\\Omega(m^{3/5-o(1)})\\).*\n\nRäty and Tomon pointed out that their methods do not extend to the range \\(2 \\le r \\le k-2\\). Obtaining strong results in the case \\(r=2\\) is of particular interest for several reasons. Firstly, it is this case that has been the most extensively studied in Theoretical Computer Science. This problem is known as *Max \\(E_k\\)-Set Splitting*, or, in the case the hypergraph is not uniform, as *Max Set Splitting*. Secondly, this case has close connections to other well-studied problems such as negative (or positive) discrepancy, bisection width and hypergraph colourings.\n\nUsing a novel approach, we prove the following approximate version of the Conlon–Fox–Kwan–Sudakov conjecture for all values of \\(r\\).\n\n**Theorem 4.** *For any \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\), there exists some \\(k_0 = k_0(\\varepsilon)\\) such that for any \\(k > k_0\\) and \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\), every \\(k\\)-uniform hypergraph with \\(m\\) edges has an \\(r\\)-cut of surplus \\(\\Omega(m^{2/3-\\varepsilon})\\).*\n\nIn the case of linear hypergraphs, still for \\(r \\in \\{k-1,k\\}\\), Räty and Tomon [6] proved a stronger bound \\(\\Omega(m^{3/4-o(1)})\\). They noted that this is asymptotically optimal, by constructing \\(k\\)-graphs with \\(r\\)-surplus \\(O(m^{3/4})\\) in which each pair of vertices is in at most \\(O(\\log m)\\) edges (i.e., the hypergraphs are nearly linear). They conjectured that the \\(o(1)\\) term can be removed and that the result can be extended to all \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\).\n\nWe completely resolve this conjecture.\n\n**Theorem 5.** *For any \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\) with either \\(k \\ge 4\\) or \\(r \\ge 3\\), every \\(k\\)-uniform linear hypergraph with \\(m\\) edges has an \\(r\\)-cut of surplus \\(\\Omega(m^{3/4})\\).*\n\nWe also show that this is tight up to a constant factor by constructing linear (not just nearly linear) \\(k\\)-graphs with \\(r\\)-surplus \\(O(m^{3/4})\\).\n\n**Proposition 6.** *Fix some integers \\(2 \\le r \\le k\\). There is a constant \\(\\alpha = \\alpha(k)\\) such that, for every \\(m \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), there exists an \\(m\\)-edge \\(k\\)-uniform linear hypergraph with \\(r\\)-surplus at most \\(\\alpha m^{3/4}\\).*--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPositive discrepancy of graphs far from Turán graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02681)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Fri Oct  2 02:03:28 2026 ... These results prove two conjectures of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon.\nTitle: Positive discrepancy of graphs far from Turán graphs\nAuthors: Leyou Xu, Bo Zhou\nDate: Fri Oct  2 02:03:28 2026\n\nWe prove that, for every $\\eps>0$, any $n$-vertex graph that needs at least $\\eps n^2$ edge changes to become a Turán graph has positive discrepancy at least $c_\\eps n^{5/4}$. Consequently, every such regular graph has second eigenvalue at least $c'_\\eps n^{1/4}$. These results prove two conjectures of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTight bounds for positive discrepancy via eigenvalues (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37961)\nciteturn2academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Tue Sep 29 16:32:59 2026 ... This confirms a conjecture of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon.\nTitle: Tight bounds for positive discrepancy via eigenvalues\nAuthors: Oliver Janzer, István Tomon, Fredy Yip\nDate: Tue Sep 29 16:32:59 2026\n\nGiven an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $M$ with largest eigenvalue $λ_1\\geq 0$, it is easy to show that the solution of the optimisation problem $\\max_{v\\in [-1,1]^n}v^TMv$ is at most $λ_1 n$. We prove the following converse: if every $n'\\times n'$ principal submatrix of $M$ has maximal eigenvalue at least $λ$, then $\\max_{v\\in [-1,1]^n}v^TMv\\geq λ(n-n'+1)$. We use this lemma to improve a number of recent results on the MaxCut, bisection width, and discrepancy of graphs. Among others, we prove that every $n$-vertex $m$-edge graph that is far from a disjoint union of cliques has a cut of size at least $m/2+n^{5/4-o(1)}$, which is sharp up to the $o(1)$-term. Moreover, we prove that every $d$-regular $n$-vertex graph has bisection width at most $dn/4-Ω_{\\varepsilon}(d^{1/3}n)$ for $d\\leq (1-\\varepsilon)n/2$, which is optimal for $d=Ω(n)$. This confirms a conjecture of Räty, Sudakov and Tomon.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIstván Tomon - List of Publications (https://sites.google.com/view/istvantomon/publications/list-of-publications)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Tomon. ... Räty, B. Sudakov, I. ... Journal of Graph Theory 112 (4) (202 6): 438-441.\nJournal publications\n\n2026\n\nN. Frankl, A. Jung, I. Tomon. The Quantitative Fractional Helly theorem. Isr. J. Math. 274 (2026): 235-253.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-025-2862-7\n\nE. Räty, B. Sudakov, I. Tomon. Positive discrepancy, MaxCut, and eigenvalues of graphs.  Trans. AMS 379 (3) (2026): 2111-2140.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1090/tran/9551\n\nZ. Hunter, A. Milojević, B. Sudakov, I. Tomon. Long induced paths in K_{s,s}-free graphs. Journal of Graph Theory 112 (4) (202 6): 438-441.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.70040\n\nA. Bishnoi, I. Tomon. Explicit constructions of optimal blocking sets and minimal codes. Combinatorica 46 (1 3) (2026).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00493-026-00202-5\n\nI. Balla, L. Hambardzumyan, I. Tomon. Factorization norms and an inverse theorem for MaxCut. Mathematische Annalen 394 (52) (202 6).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00208-026-03355-2also in: In Proceedings of the Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (FOCS) (2025): 947–963. IEEE.\n\nV. Falgas-Ravry, E. Räty, I. Tomon. Dedekind's problem in the hypergrid. Adv. Math. 488 (2026): 110796.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2026.110796\n\nE. Räty, I. Tomon. Bisection Width, Discrepancy, and Eigenvalues of Hypergraphs. Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B 177 (2026): 186-215.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2025.11.003\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------